#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157069 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7043" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ee7ef0a95e618121f4e0653c6beaa64248682ab524ecee8903acec89827e77d.png)\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](images/03/031dd658d772ef4184ceb173311102f64bd0ff3ff5e34321ad4648e0d5026383.png)\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/d8/d82f994f6426a091fb6fc46b2b2b6fbc66d0b94e7ed1ead3e67257292f4c985b.png)*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/b1/b1ba76ba62019524549b4bcf4c36ec2440b2db8f585150a9456218972363a215.png) \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/f8/f86bdf3e241a51548f8c58fe739e11071197944f5fe2361f83bb34408333211c.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/9b/9b2628d7d3c88303bdd7ed2a8aadcdcfe751a01d910f64972a60cc080d039181.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157070 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-7044" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/4d/4d932848b1d30448dd0135bc75ded935f2065037d4964423899013a47c7a526a.png)\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/a4/a474b7882cfa32e343d1781a731a4eb540994f72730320d7b8a61639e7e86a14.png)*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/60/60dd7b0b483c745b0ffda2c89d3216ab463cab5870f91b58b54c7f8e4e446cfd.png)\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/9e/9e4e860107360ac18ce72e7f4f07dbf9106de041dea3b6c9228cc30bc45c714e.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/17/175d9bde8dda1bfb3a653521add06d23944d72019c0718eed6407abe97f2e87c.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/12/12f33df5f036901c6069ee03a9ba8ccb05cde1071b7add3d1b310da12bb8298d.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157071 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "53243f33-77a1-59f1-bce9-3f084e087593" :question/math-academy-id "e-7045" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6acacc8b2c54be234e536c373c40f4b5b53646fe6091181672c06e437f5366.png)\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](images/14/14c8e142a4fc541b8da91c6bcd1c8517451b436b7f8c677bfb239ca4bfa79588.png)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/47/471ff0aa856363c49a494f0e711dec8558b429261ddd99685bd0a19416264d05.png) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =1+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](images/9f/9fbccda402b330b568828c47040f1867c0b075ff434f6d94c2d5d5bcea85f4a8.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/91/91e10c00aed02305fdb5afd421aad57fe52143e3305d84da00819e62f2b30a14.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 1+\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/2c/2ccc9c4cb7dd95e168690c017e8fca6f92315d1759b3e3ec6bb3a82551b39e3c.png)\n\nWe now separate this solution into its real and imaginary parts. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ } \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "9bd3af1e-e569-5cf1-8c4e-97b9ecc35174" :question/math-academy-id "e-7046" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/08/082bb18706b8bae20dbc5b8531829f3b8523d9262c2fd0f7c4be78a3b2ef4ebb.png)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/65/657dced55da3afc2c15487267793aaf282e8c258ca5e7556bacecff58a496d82.png) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =3+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](images/9f/9fbccda402b330b568828c47040f1867c0b075ff434f6d94c2d5d5bcea85f4a8.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/5d/5d9c13b213c1e44a755000997c61f5ba5a3c91159eb7a5e6a574acf34019efab.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 3 + \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/38/38fd775722fd8ce56abb0f09499bb0a268ae73b9483a182d4564381557d190cf.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/f2/f2282999a9f221bd9a14118c116c573f82521e6140d24b008b6fea04c5a61fcf.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a5dad7-c94f-51da-a677-ef81f5bf32cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302815} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac8dcd5-8feb-5327-aa14-8382a8c89d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302822} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec9adaa-1031-5be5-9bbc-b931868084e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302950} {:answer/id #uuid "522ee2e1-5f6a-5437-9867-bb0bb77e89b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302966} {:answer/id #uuid "753eaf97-31e8-58ae-bd6a-f193b0665738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ac8dcd5-8feb-5327-aa14-8382a8c89d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302822} :answer-field/id #uuid "33ba3425-036d-579b-bf3d-66b6d684a1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284bef67-027d-5606-82aa-6c0a65dd3c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-328592" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f0bc03-23e9-5844-a152-810bdf973b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305216} {:answer/id #uuid "27809fcd-4be6-5bac-9d33-62f6d12f9c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305520} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f36198-3538-55ec-8148-7d6978fe6964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306659} {:answer/id #uuid "f5517b5c-9b51-5048-865f-4559006210d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307421} {:answer/id #uuid "f7735b63-f11b-54fb-adbc-dc86dab8bb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f36198-3538-55ec-8148-7d6978fe6964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306659} :answer-field/id #uuid "44687a24-fd41-5a07-bdd7-e77e03c1b3f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d09bcde9-8cbf-534e-b009-ecb0c5d97dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-334063" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 26 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 26 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+0[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13bf71d5-573c-52c9-bf24-51784fb7ec1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305320} {:answer/id #uuid "7aadca5c-f8ed-5bdb-8b77-9d0af1f08f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306322} {:answer/id #uuid "8876c1a9-7b74-582b-8d23-4ff3010aa56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306457} {:answer/id #uuid "887792ce-2fac-54c5-85fa-1d7a23cb92d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306458} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fa5a20-7df1-5a16-bdd6-e6bcfdab025d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4fa5a20-7df1-5a16-bdd6-e6bcfdab025d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306986} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1240bd8-a5f5-5f09-a6c2-bec2bc9728d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ae1104-266d-589e-8326-583eecd00f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-334103" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ f(t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}]}
